{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdCVqzrodhdJJdQEoAfd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate and it passes the self-check (`OK`). The check didn't test novelty against the other models, and its one-chain smoke fit doesn't show whether the full fit will converge.\n\n**Model name:** `satisficing_first_look_acceptance`\n\n**Hypothesis:** people often settle for the first sequence that looks good enough instead of always comparing the two. Each person usually starts with one side, and how likely they are to start on the left is a habit of their own. If the sequence they look at first already seems random enough by their own personal standard, they pick it without weighing the other one. Only when it falls short do they compare the two. How random each sequence looks is judged exactly as in the current best model, `asymmetric_tolerance_lopsided_stretch`, and people still guess on some trials.\n\n**Why this slot:** this is an exploratory slot, and the change is to the decision process, not to how randomness is perceived. It targets the critique's one flagged problem: people differ more in how often they choose Left than the best model produces (p=0.016, though it doesn't survive the FDR correction, q=0.128). The two side-bias models already in the set add a fixed tilt toward one button. Here the side preference comes from lenient people stopping at the first acceptable sequence. That gives three predictions they don't make:\n- The pull toward a side depends on how random the first sequence looks on its own, not only on the difference between the two.\n- People with strict standards show almost no side bias.\n- If everyone's standard were very high, the model would reduce to the best model.\n\n**Check results:**\n- The smoke fit's ELPD-LOO was −3499.1, against −3500.3 for the best model. That's essentially level and only a short fit, so it isn't evidence of an improvement.\n- T